course: religion-ethics
lesson: 31
title: Islam
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Islam is a faith in one God, called Allah in Arabic; its followers are Muslims, and the word Islam means "submission", giving your life to God.
explain: Muslims believe God gave his message to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) in Arabia in the 600s, and that this message is written in the Qur'an.
explain: Muslims live their faith every day through the Five Pillars: saying their faith, praying five times a day, giving to the poor, fasting in Ramadan and, if they can, making the pilgrimage to Mecca.
explain: Christians and Muslims share a lot: one God, prayer, care for the poor, and great respect for Jesus, whom Muslims call Isa and honour as a prophet, though not as the Son of God.
explain: For a Christian child today this means you may have Muslim friends who fast, pray and keep halal food, and the right thing is to learn what they really believe and never to judge them by the worst things a few people do.
fact: Islam teaches belief in one God, called Allah in Arabic.
fact: Followers of Islam are called Muslims, and Islam is the second largest religion in the world.
fact: Muslims believe Muhammad is the last prophet, and many say "peace be upon him" after his name.
fact: Muslims do not make pictures of the Prophet Muhammad, as a sign of respect.
fact: The Qur'an is the holy book of Islam; Muslims believe it is God's word, and it is recited in Arabic.
fact: The Five Pillars are: the declaration of faith, prayer five times a day, giving to the poor (zakat), fasting in Ramadan and the pilgrimage to Mecca (Hajj).
fact: A mosque is the Muslim place of prayer; people take off their shoes and pray side by side in rows facing Mecca, the holy city of Islam, led by an imam; standing shoulder to shoulder shows that everyone is equal before God.
fact: In the month of Ramadan, Muslims fast from dawn to sunset; young children, the sick and travellers do not have to fast.
fact: Eid al-Fitr is the joyful festival at the end of Ramadan.
fact: Halal means allowed; halal food has no pork and Muslims do not drink alcohol.
fact: Muslims honour Jesus (Isa) as a great prophet born of the virgin Mary, but do not believe he is the Son of God.
fact: Islamophobia is fear or hatred of Muslims; most Muslims reject violence done in the name of Islam.
exercise: What do Muslims call God in Arabic? | Allah | Isa | Hajj | Zakat
exercise: What does the word Islam mean? | Submission to God | The holy city | The festival of light | Peace upon him
exercise: Who do Muslims believe is the last prophet? | Muhammad | Moses | Abraham | Guru Nanak
exercise: Why do many Muslims say "peace be upon him" after Muhammad's name? | To show him respect | To mark the start of a prayer | To end the fast at sunset | To greet a friend in the street
exercise: What is the holy book of Islam? | The Qur'an | The Torah | The Guru Granth Sahib | The Vedas
exercise: In which language is the Qur'an recited? | Arabic | Hebrew | Latin | Punjabi
exercise: Which of these is one of the Five Pillars of Islam? | Giving to the poor | Lighting candles at Diwali | Wearing a steel bracelet | Going to Mass on Sunday
exercise: How many times a day do Muslims pray? | Five | Three | Seven | Once
exercise: Where do Muslims travel on the Hajj pilgrimage? | Mecca | Jerusalem | Rome | Amritsar
exercise: What do people do before praying in a mosque? | Take off their shoes | Light a lamp | Ring a bell | Put on a crown
exercise: Who leads prayer in a mosque? | The imam | The priest | The rabbi | The guru
exercise: When do Muslims fast during Ramadan? | From dawn to sunset | All day and all night | Only on Fridays | From sunset to dawn
exercise: What is Eid al-Fitr? | The festival at the end of Ramadan | The first day of the pilgrimage to Mecca | The Muslim new year's fast | The day the Qur'an is printed
exercise: Which food is not halal? | Pork | Chicken | Rice | Lamb
exercise: What do Muslims believe about Jesus? | He was a great prophet, not the Son of God | He was the Son of God and rose again | He was an ordinary teacher, not a prophet | He was never a real person, only a story told later
exercise: Why do Muslims not make pictures of the Prophet? | Out of respect for him | Because he asked to be forgotten | Because pictures were not invented | Because his face was a secret
exercise: What is Islamophobia? | Fear or hatred of Muslims | A Muslim prayer for peace | A fast during Ramadan | A rule about halal food
exercise: Why do Muslims pray side by side in rows? | It shows everyone is equal before God | It keeps the rich and important people at the front | It lets the imam count how many people have come | It is the only way to fit everyone into the room
test: my cousin says Muslims worship a different God called Allah, like a made-up name. right? | No, Allah is simply the Arabic word for God | Yes, Allah is the name of a prophet | Yes, Allah is a god worshipped only by Arab people | No, Allah is the name of their holy book
test: Yusuf from my class asks: "Do Christians respect Jesus more than we do?" What is a fair answer? | Both honour Jesus; Christians believe he is God's Son, Muslims a prophet | Muslims do not believe Jesus existed at all | Christians and Muslims believe exactly the same about Jesus | Only Christians have ever heard of Jesus
test: bro reckons Muslims fast the whole month, no food at all for 30 days lol. true? | No, they eat after sunset and before dawn | Yes, nothing at all for the whole month | No, they only fast on the very last day of the month | Yes, but they may drink milk all day
test: Amira won't eat the pepperoni pizza at your party. Why most likely? | Pork is not halal, so she does not eat it | She is fasting for Christmas | Muslims never eat at parties | She thinks pizza is unhealthy
test: some kid in the playground: "all Muslims are dangerous, it's on the news." What is the best reply? | Most Muslims reject violence; judging all by a few is unfair | You're right, the news is always fair about everyone and every group | Only some Muslims, the ones who pray a lot | It doesn't matter, it's not our business to say
test: You visit a mosque with your class. What should you do at the door? | Take your shoes off, as the worshippers do | Keep your shoes on to show you are a guest | Ring the bell to say you have come | Bow to the imam three times
test: Sam says the Qur'an is just a book about Muhammad's life. Is he right? | No, Muslims believe it is God's own word to Muhammad | Yes, it is his diary written by himself | Yes, it was written by his friends for fun | No, it is a book of Arabic poems only
test: It is Ramadan and your friend Bilal is 6. Must he fast all day? | No, young children do not have to fast | Yes, every Muslim must fast from birth | Yes, but only if his parents are fasting | No, only the imam fasts in Ramadan
test: olympiad judge: "Name two things Christians and Muslims share." | Belief in one God and care for the poor | The same holy book and the same festivals | Belief that Jesus is God's Son and Easter | Praying five times a day and Hajj
test: why do they all pray facing one way in the mosque? | They face Mecca, the holy city of Islam | They face the imam's house, which is next to the mosque | They face the sunrise every time, wherever they are | They face the biggest window to see the sky
test: What is zakat? | Giving part of your money to the poor | A journey to the holy city | A prayer said at sunrise | The meal after the fast
test: shut up, Eid is like Muslim Christmas, same thing. Is that right? | Not quite; Eid al-Fitr ends the Ramadan fast | Yes, Eid celebrates the birth of Jesus, just like Christmas | Yes, both are on the 25th of December every single year | No, Eid is a sad day of fasting with no food or presents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Muslim understanding of the Qur'an? | It is held to be the word of God, revealed to the Prophet Muhammad | It is held to be a history of the Arab peoples composed by scholars | It is held to be a collection of the sayings of Jesus | It is held to be a book of laws devised by the imams
exam: Identify the enumeration that corresponds to the Five Pillars of Islam. | Declaration of faith, prayer, almsgiving, fasting and pilgrimage | Baptism, communion, confession, marriage and prayer | Prayer, meditation, vegetarianism, service and pilgrimage | Fasting, feasting, singing, reading and giving
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the observance of Ramadan. | Adult Muslims abstain from food and drink between dawn and sunset | All Muslims abstain from food for the entirety of the month | Muslims abstain from food solely upon Fridays | Muslims abstain from food between sunset and dawn
exam: Ascertain the position held in Islam in respect of Jesus of Nazareth. | He is honoured as a prophet but not as the Son of God | He is regarded as the Son of God and Saviour | He is regarded as having never existed | He is regarded as the founder of Islam
